# T-1258/T-2356: this repo cut over to ledger v2 (disjoint tickets/T-####/
# directories, ordinary git objects git's native per-file 3-way merge
# already resolves correctly). tickets.md/tickets-archive.md and the
# frob-ledger merge driver they needed are gone -- see
# docs/design/ledger-v2.md#7-reversible-migration-plan-design-for-the-child-ticket-not-built-here.
# The merge driver itself (`frob ticket merge-driver`, splice_ledger) is
# NOT removed from frob's own source -- other repos using frob may still
# be on v1/monofile mode during their own compatibility window; only
# THIS repo's now-inert data files and the .gitattributes lines that
# routed them through the driver are retired here.

# T-1873: rapid-debt.jsonl is a tracked, append-only ledger -- every rapid
# -profile land appends one record to its tail, and concurrent worktrees
# appending near the same line hit git's default line-level merge, which
# reports a false textual conflict on a file whose whole value is that no
# record is ever lost (agents were hand-editing it to resolve this, which
# is exactly how a record silently disappears). git's BUILT-IN `merge=
# union` driver is preferred over a new frob driver here: union is
# exactly append-only "keep both sides" semantics, and unlike `merge=
# frob-ledger` above it needs NO per-clone `git config` registration --
# a worktree that skipped that one-time setup silently falls back to the
# default (conflicting) driver, which is exactly the failure mode this
# ticket exists to close. Verified by reproduction (tests/unit/
# test_gitattributes_merge.py): two branches each appending a different
# record merge with both records surviving and zero conflict markers.
# T-0480: anchored with a leading slash for the same reason `/tickets.md`
# is above -- an unanchored pattern would also match any other file named
# `rapid-debt.jsonl` (or `force-overrides.jsonl`) anywhere in the tree.
# force-overrides.jsonl (frob.tickets._force_override, T-1762) is the
# same tracked-root-append-only shape -- covered here even though no
# `--force` override has happened yet in this repo (the file does not
# exist on disk), so the FIRST concurrent append is already covered
# rather than conflicting once, then getting a follow-up fix.
/rapid-debt.jsonl merge=union
/force-overrides.jsonl merge=union

# T-2586: this repo's own `core.autocrlf=true` (a Windows-checkout
# setting, present on this Linux/WSL clone) converts LF to CRLF on
# checkout for any tracked text file with no explicit `text`/`eol`
# attribute -- `git check-attr text eol -- rapid-debt.jsonl` read both
# "unspecified" before this line. Every deferred rapid-profile sweep
# rewrites `rapid-debt.jsonl` with LF-only content; without a pinned
# attribute, `autocrlf` reintroduces CRLF on the next checkout, and the
# resulting byte-count mismatch against the LF-only index blob is
# exactly what fooled `git status`'s stat-comparison fast path into a
# false "modified" report (root_dirt's own content-confirmation fix in
# this same ticket is the second, independent layer -- this line
# removes the CAUSE; that one makes the report correct regardless of
# cause). `eol=lf` pins this file to LF on checkout AND normalizes CRLF
# to LF on commit, matching the `merge=union` line immediately above --
# both attributes are declared HERE, in the tracked repo, rather than
# left to a per-clone `git config core.autocrlf=false`, because a config
# change does not travel with the repo and a clone that skips it
# silently falls back to the broken behavior (the same argument that
# chose a built-in merge driver over a per-clone git config for the
# `merge=union` line above).
/rapid-debt.jsonl text eol=lf
/force-overrides.jsonl text eol=lf

# T-1433 incident: attachment shas are byte-exact; CRLF conversion breaks COV004.
# T-2239: T-1433's glob only matched the OLD v1 flat layout (tickets/attachments/**).
# Ledger v2 stores attachments per-ticket (tickets/<id>/attachments/**), a path shape
# the v1 glob never matches, so v2 attachments were still CRLF-converted on checkout,
# desyncing their on-disk sha256 from the sha256 recorded at attach time (LF content).
# Both layouts are covered here -- v1 attachments still exist on disk (tickets/
# attachments/T-1433/...) and must not regress.
tickets/attachments/** -text
tickets/*/attachments/** -text
